"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 13.5 and an ISUP grade group 2 finding on the prostatectomy specimen (Gleason 3+4). Positive prognostic indicators include negative surgical margins, no extraprostatic extension, and no seminal vesicle invasion documented in the pathology report. Conversely, the absence of nodal sampling widens uncertainty regarding nodal status. The biopsy findings (ISUP grade group 1) differ from the prostatectomy specimen grade (ISUP grade group 2).\n\nInformation that would significantly revise this prognosis includes postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trajectory over time. Further pathological data, such as definitive nodal status, would also refine risk assessment. Given the current documentation, the uncertainty surrounding nodal involvement is a key limitation. The fixed estimated horizon is 69.39 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 41.64–97.15 months; its coverage has not been validated."
